/* CSO Cyber Range — Course content: IC-206 Applied Cryptography & PKI Management */
window.COURSE_DATA = window.COURSE_DATA || {};
window.COURSE_DATA["IC-206"] = {
ov:"Cryptography underpins nearly every security control, and misapplying it silently breaks the protection it promises. This operational course builds applied cryptography and PKI: the primitives and what they provide, certificate authority hierarchies, TLS hardening, key lifecycle management, and the failures that defeat otherwise-strong crypto. Each module ends in a required knowledge check; the 40-item capstone draws two items per module plus applied scenarios.",
mods:[
{t:"Cryptography Fundamentals and Goals",d:"What cryptography actually provides.",
obj:["State the security goals cryptography provides","Distinguish confidentiality, integrity, authentication, non-repudiation","Explain what cryptography does not solve"],
read:["Cryptography provides four security goals that map onto the security properties from BC-101: confidentiality (encryption keeps data secret), integrity (hashing and MACs detect alteration), authentication (proving identity or origin), and non-repudiation (digital signatures prevent denial of an action). Each goal is served by specific primitives — encryption for confidentiality, hashing for integrity, signatures for authentication and non-repudiation — and a common error is assuming one primitive provides a goal it does not (encryption provides confidentiality but not integrity; a hash provides integrity but not confidentiality). Knowing which primitive provides which goal is the foundation of applying cryptography correctly.",
"Equally important is knowing what cryptography does not solve. It protects data, but a system with strong encryption and weak access control, poor key management, or exploitable software is not secure — cryptography is one control among many (the defense-in-depth lesson). Crypto also does not make endpoints trustworthy: encrypted data is decrypted to be used, so a compromised endpoint sees the plaintext. And crypto is only as strong as its implementation and key management — the recurring theme of this course is that cryptography fails not because the math is broken but because it is misapplied, misconfigured, or its keys are mishandled. Understanding the goals and the limits frames everything that follows."],
ex:"A team encrypts data in transit but assumes this also guarantees integrity, and an attacker who cannot read the encrypted data still manages to alter it in ways that cause harm (a bit-flipping attack on a mode without integrity protection). Encryption provided confidentiality but not integrity — the team needed authenticated encryption (a MAC or AEAD mode) to get both. Assuming one primitive provided a goal it did not was the failure, exactly the misapplication this course guards against.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-175B","Guideline for using cryptographic standards — goals and mechanisms."],["Ferguson, Schneier & Kohno, Cryptography Engineering, Ch.1-2","Cryptographic goals and their proper application."]],
kc:[
{q:"Which cryptographic goal does a hash function alone provide?",o:["Confidentiality","Integrity — detecting alteration","Non-repudiation","Key exchange"],a:1,x:"Hashing detects modification (integrity) but does not hide data (confidentiality) or prove origin (authentication) by itself."},
{q:"Encryption alone provides confidentiality but not:",o:["Secrecy","Integrity — it does not by itself detect alteration; authenticated encryption is needed for both","Speed","Compression"],a:1,x:"A common error is assuming encryption guarantees integrity; without a MAC/AEAD, encrypted data can be altered undetectably."},
{q:"Cryptography does NOT solve which problem?",o:["Data confidentiality","Making a compromised endpoint trustworthy — plaintext is exposed once decrypted for use","Data integrity","Origin authentication"],a:1,x:"Crypto is one control among many; a compromised endpoint sees the decrypted plaintext regardless of encryption strength."}]},
{t:"Symmetric Encryption",d:"AES and shared-key confidentiality.",
obj:["Explain symmetric encryption and its use of a shared key","Describe AES and modes of operation","Explain the key distribution problem"],
read:["Symmetric encryption uses the same key to encrypt and decrypt, and it is the workhorse of bulk data confidentiality because it is fast. AES (Advanced Encryption Standard) is the dominant algorithm, with 128- or 256-bit keys, and it is trusted and efficient. But a block cipher like AES only encrypts fixed-size blocks, so a mode of operation governs how it encrypts larger data — and the mode choice is security-critical. ECB mode (encrypting each block independently) is insecure because identical plaintext blocks produce identical ciphertext, leaking patterns; secure modes like GCM (which also provides integrity — authenticated encryption) or CTR are required.",
"Symmetric encryption's fundamental challenge is key distribution: both parties need the same secret key, but how do they share it securely without an attacker intercepting it? For two parties who have never communicated, symmetric crypto alone cannot solve this — you cannot send the key over the same insecure channel you are trying to protect. This is exactly the problem asymmetric cryptography (next module) solves, and in practice systems combine both: asymmetric crypto to securely establish a symmetric key, then fast symmetric crypto for the bulk data. Understanding symmetric encryption's speed advantage and its key-distribution limitation motivates the hybrid approach that real protocols like TLS use."],
ex:"A developer encrypts an image with AES in ECB mode, and the encrypted result still visibly shows the outline of the original image — because identical blocks (regions of the same color) produced identical ciphertext, leaking the pattern. Switching to GCM mode (authenticated encryption) both hides the pattern and adds integrity. The mode choice, not the algorithm, was the failure — AES was fine, but ECB leaked structure.",
refs:[["NIST FIPS 197 (AES) and SP 800-38 series (modes)","AES and secure modes of operation."],["Cryptography Engineering, Ch.4","Block ciphers and modes."]],
kc:[
{q:"Symmetric encryption uses:",o:["Different keys for encrypt and decrypt","The same key to encrypt and decrypt, making it fast for bulk data","No key","A public and private key"],a:1,x:"Symmetric crypto shares one key and is fast, ideal for bulk data — but faces the key-distribution problem."},
{q:"ECB mode is insecure because:",o:["It is too slow","Identical plaintext blocks produce identical ciphertext, leaking patterns","It uses no key","It is not standardized"],a:1,x:"ECB encrypts blocks independently, so structure in the plaintext leaks into the ciphertext; secure modes like GCM avoid this."},
{q:"The fundamental challenge of symmetric encryption is:",o:["It is too fast","Key distribution — securely sharing the same secret key between parties","It cannot encrypt","It is insecure"],a:1,x:"Both parties need the same key, but sharing it securely over an insecure channel is the problem asymmetric crypto solves."}]},
{t:"Asymmetric Encryption",d:"RSA, ECC, and public-key cryptography.",
obj:["Explain asymmetric encryption and key pairs","Describe RSA and ECC and their uses","Explain how asymmetric crypto solves key distribution"],
read:["Asymmetric (public-key) cryptography uses a mathematically-related key pair: a public key that can be shared freely and a private key kept secret. Data encrypted with the public key can only be decrypted with the private key, and — crucially for signatures — data signed with the private key can be verified with the public key. RSA (based on the difficulty of factoring large numbers) and ECC (Elliptic Curve Cryptography, based on the elliptic-curve discrete-log problem, offering equivalent security with smaller keys) are the dominant algorithms. Asymmetric crypto is slower than symmetric, so it is used for key establishment and signatures, not bulk data.",
"Asymmetric cryptography solves the key-distribution problem that symmetric crypto cannot: two parties who have never met can communicate securely because one can encrypt to the other's freely-shared public key, and only the holder of the corresponding private key can decrypt. This is the foundation of PKI and TLS. In practice, systems use asymmetric crypto to securely establish a shared symmetric key (or via key exchange, module six), then switch to fast symmetric encryption for the data — the hybrid approach. The public/private key pair also underpins digital signatures (module five) and certificates (module seven). Asymmetric cryptography is thus the enabler of secure communication between strangers, which is the whole internet's security model."],
ex:"A client wants to send data securely to a server it has never contacted. It obtains the server's public key (from its certificate), encrypts a symmetric session key with it, and sends it — only the server, holding the matching private key, can decrypt it. Now both share a symmetric key for fast bulk encryption. Asymmetric crypto solved the key distribution that symmetric alone could not, enabling secure communication between strangers.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-56B (RSA) and FIPS 186 (ECC/signatures)","Asymmetric cryptography standards."],["Cryptography Engineering, Ch.12-13","Public-key encryption and its uses."]],
kc:[
{q:"Asymmetric cryptography uses:",o:["One shared secret key","A key pair — a freely-shared public key and a secret private key","No keys","Only symmetric keys"],a:1,x:"The public/private key pair enables encryption to a public key (decryptable only by the private key) and signatures."},
{q:"ECC offers equivalent security to RSA with:",o:["Larger keys","Smaller keys, based on the elliptic-curve discrete-log problem","No keys","Weaker security"],a:1,x:"ECC achieves comparable security with smaller keys, making it efficient — increasingly preferred over RSA."},
{q:"Asymmetric cryptography solves key distribution by:",o:["Sharing the private key","Letting parties who never met communicate securely — encrypting to a freely-shared public key that only the private-key holder can decrypt","Using no keys","Being faster than symmetric"],a:1,x:"Public keys can be shared openly; only the private-key holder can decrypt, enabling secure communication between strangers."}]},
{t:"Hash Functions and Integrity",d:"Fingerprinting data to detect change.",
obj:["Explain cryptographic hash functions and their properties","Describe integrity verification with hashes","Explain why some hash algorithms are broken"],
read:["A cryptographic hash function produces a fixed-size fingerprint (digest) of any input, with key properties: deterministic (same input, same hash), one-way (infeasible to reverse the hash to the input), and collision-resistant (infeasible to find two inputs with the same hash). These properties make hashes ideal for integrity verification — if the hash of data matches a known-good hash, the data is unaltered (the evidence-integrity use from BC-110), and for password storage (BC-104, with salting and slow hashing). SHA-256 and SHA-3 are current standards; the hash is the fingerprint that reveals any change.",
"Hash algorithms can be broken, and using a broken one silently undermines the security it is supposed to provide. MD5 and SHA-1 are cryptographically broken — collisions can be found, meaning an attacker can craft two different inputs with the same hash, defeating integrity and signature schemes that rely on collision resistance. Using MD5 or SHA-1 for security purposes today is a serious vulnerability, yet legacy systems and lazy implementations still do. The lesson connects to the broader theme: cryptography fails through misapplication, and using a deprecated, broken algorithm is a common misapplication that looks fine (it still produces a hash) but provides no real security. Choosing current, unbroken algorithms is a basic but frequently-violated requirement."],
ex:"A system verifies software integrity using MD5 hashes, but an attacker crafts a malicious file with the same MD5 hash as a legitimate one (an MD5 collision), passing the integrity check. Because MD5's collision resistance is broken, the integrity verification provided no real protection. Switching to SHA-256 restores meaningful integrity — the algorithm choice was the vulnerability, not the concept of hash-based integrity.",
refs:[["NIST FIPS 180-4 (SHA-2) and FIPS 202 (SHA-3)","Current hash standards."],["NIST guidance deprecating MD5 and SHA-1","Why broken hashes must not be used for security."]],
kc:[
{q:"A cryptographic hash function's key security properties are:",o:["Reversible and fast","One-way and collision-resistant — infeasible to reverse or to find two inputs with the same hash","Encrypting and decrypting","Using a shared key"],a:1,x:"One-wayness and collision resistance make hashes suitable for integrity verification and password storage."},
{q:"Using MD5 or SHA-1 for security today is:",o:["Best practice","A serious vulnerability — their collision resistance is broken, defeating integrity and signature schemes","Faster and fine","Required"],a:1,x:"Broken collision resistance lets attackers craft colliding inputs; MD5/SHA-1 provide no real security despite still producing a hash."},
{q:"Hash-based integrity verification works by:",o:["Encrypting the data","Comparing the data's hash to a known-good hash — a match means unaltered","Reversing the hash","Sharing a key"],a:1,x:"If the recomputed hash matches the trusted hash, the data is unchanged — the basis of integrity checks and evidence handling."}]},
{t:"Digital Signatures and Message Authentication",d:"Proving origin and integrity together.",
obj:["Explain digital signatures and what they provide","Distinguish signatures from MACs","Explain non-repudiation"],
read:["Digital signatures combine hashing and asymmetric cryptography to provide integrity, authentication, and non-repudiation together. To sign, you hash the data and encrypt the hash with your private key; anyone with your public key can verify by decrypting the signature and comparing to the data's hash. A valid signature proves the data is unaltered (integrity), came from the private-key holder (authentication), and — because only that holder has the private key — that they cannot later deny signing it (non-repudiation). Signatures underpin certificates, code signing, and secure email, establishing trust in origin and integrity across the internet.",
"Message Authentication Codes (MACs) provide integrity and authentication too, but with a shared symmetric key rather than a key pair. A MAC (like HMAC) lets parties who share a secret key verify that a message is unaltered and from the other party — faster than signatures but without non-repudiation (either party could have created the MAC, since both hold the key). The distinction matters: use a MAC when parties share a key and need fast integrity/authentication (as in TLS records); use a signature when you need non-repudiation or when verifiers should not be able to forge (as in certificates and code signing). Confusing them — using a MAC where non-repudiation is required — is a design error. Authenticated encryption (AEAD, like AES-GCM) combines confidentiality and a MAC in one construction, which is why it is the modern default."],
ex:"Software is distributed with a digital signature: the vendor signs the hash of the software with their private key, and users verify with the vendor's public key. A valid signature proves the software is unaltered and genuinely from the vendor (and the vendor cannot deny it — non-repudiation), so a tampered or forged package fails verification. Had the vendor used a shared-key MAC instead, anyone with the key could forge it and the vendor could deny it — signatures were required for this trust model.",
refs:[["NIST FIPS 186-5 (digital signatures) and SP 800-38B (MACs)","Signature and MAC standards."],["Cryptography Engineering, Ch.7 & 14","MACs, signatures, and their proper use."]],
kc:[
{q:"A digital signature provides:",o:["Only confidentiality","Integrity, authentication, and non-repudiation — proving unaltered origin that the signer cannot deny","Only speed","Key exchange"],a:1,x:"Signing a hash with a private key, verifiable by the public key, proves origin and integrity with non-repudiation."},
{q:"A MAC differs from a digital signature in that a MAC:",o:["Uses a key pair","Uses a shared symmetric key and provides no non-repudiation, since either party could create it","Is slower","Provides confidentiality"],a:1,x:"MACs use a shared key for fast integrity/authentication but lack non-repudiation; signatures use a key pair and provide it."},
{q:"You need non-repudiation, so you must use:",o:["A MAC","A digital signature — a MAC cannot prove which party created it since both hold the key","A hash","Symmetric encryption"],a:1,x:"Non-repudiation requires that only the signer could have produced it — a private-key signature, not a shared-key MAC."}]},
{t:"Key Exchange and Forward Secrecy",d:"Establishing shared secrets securely.",
obj:["Explain the Diffie-Hellman key exchange concept","Explain perfect forward secrecy","Describe ephemeral key exchange"],
read:["Key exchange lets two parties establish a shared secret over an insecure channel without ever transmitting the secret itself — the Diffie-Hellman (DH) protocol and its elliptic-curve variant (ECDH) allow each party to combine their own private value with the other's public value to independently compute the same shared secret, which an eavesdropper cannot derive from the exchanged public values. This is how TLS establishes the symmetric session key without an attacker learning it, an elegant solution to the key-distribution problem that complements asymmetric encryption.",
"Perfect forward secrecy (PFS) is a critical property provided by ephemeral key exchange: using fresh, temporary key-exchange values for each session means that even if a server's long-term private key is later compromised, past recorded sessions cannot be decrypted, because the session keys were derived from ephemeral values that no longer exist. Without PFS, an attacker who records encrypted traffic and later steals the server's private key can decrypt all of it retroactively — a serious risk given long-term data sensitivity and the reality of eventual key compromise. Modern TLS mandates ephemeral key exchange (ECDHE) precisely to provide PFS. This is why key-exchange configuration matters: choosing ephemeral over static key exchange protects the confidentiality of past communications against future key compromise, a subtle but important hardening decision."],
ex:"An attacker records an organization's encrypted TLS traffic for months, then compromises the server and steals its private key. Because the server used ephemeral key exchange (ECDHE) providing perfect forward secrecy, the recorded past sessions still cannot be decrypted — their session keys came from ephemeral values that are gone. Without PFS (static key exchange), the stolen key would have decrypted everything recorded. The ephemeral-key configuration protected past communications against the future compromise.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-56A (key establishment)","Diffie-Hellman and key-exchange schemes."],["Cryptography Engineering, Ch.11","Key exchange and forward secrecy."]],
kc:[
{q:"Diffie-Hellman key exchange lets two parties:",o:["Transmit the secret key openly","Establish a shared secret over an insecure channel without transmitting the secret itself","Skip encryption","Share private keys"],a:1,x:"Each party combines their private value with the other's public value to compute the same secret an eavesdropper cannot derive."},
{q:"Perfect forward secrecy means:",o:["Keys never expire","Even if the long-term private key is later compromised, past recorded sessions cannot be decrypted","No encryption is needed","Keys are shared"],a:1,x:"Ephemeral session keys mean a future key compromise cannot retroactively decrypt past traffic — a critical protection."},
{q:"Modern TLS mandates ephemeral key exchange (ECDHE) to:",o:["Speed up the handshake","Provide perfect forward secrecy, protecting past sessions against future key compromise","Avoid certificates","Use static keys"],a:1,x:"Ephemeral key exchange gives PFS; without it, a later-stolen private key decrypts all previously recorded traffic."}]},
{t:"Public Key Infrastructure Architecture",d:"The trust framework for certificates.",
obj:["Explain the purpose and components of PKI","Describe how certificates bind identity to keys","Explain the trust model"],
read:["Public Key Infrastructure (PKI) solves a critical problem: how do you know a public key really belongs to who it claims to? Anyone can generate a key pair and claim to be your bank; PKI provides the trust framework that binds public keys to verified identities through certificates. A digital certificate is a document, signed by a trusted Certificate Authority (CA), that binds a public key to an identity (a domain, an organization, a person) — so when you receive a certificate, you can verify the CA's signature and trust that the public key belongs to the named identity. PKI's components are the CAs, the certificates, the registration authorities that verify identity, and the mechanisms for issuing, managing, and revoking certificates.",
"The trust model is hierarchical and rests on trusted roots: your system (browser, OS) ships with a set of trusted root CA certificates, and it trusts any certificate that chains up to one of those roots through valid signatures. This lets you trust a certificate from a CA you have never directly interacted with, because it chains to a root you do trust — the reciprocity-of-trust that scales the model to the whole internet. PKI is the foundation of TLS (the padlock in your browser), code signing, secure email, and much more. Its trust is only as strong as the CAs (a compromised or careless CA can issue fraudulent certificates) and the private-key protection at every level, which is why PKI management is a serious discipline."],
ex:"A browser connects to a bank's website and receives its certificate. The browser verifies that the certificate is signed by an intermediate CA, whose certificate is signed by a root CA in the browser's trusted store, and that the certificate's domain matches — establishing trust that the public key really belongs to the bank. PKI's chain of trust let the browser trust a bank it never pre-configured, because the certificate chained to a trusted root — the model that scales to the whole web.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-32 and RFC 5280","PKI concepts and X.509 certificates."],["Cryptography Engineering, Ch.19","PKI and certificate trust."]],
kc:[
{q:"A digital certificate binds:",o:["Two symmetric keys","A public key to a verified identity, signed by a trusted Certificate Authority","A password to a user","Nothing"],a:1,x:"Certificates, signed by a CA, bind a public key to an identity so you can trust the key belongs to who it claims."},
{q:"The PKI trust model rests on:",o:["Trusting every certificate","Trusted root CAs — your system trusts any certificate chaining up to a root it trusts","No trust at all","Symmetric keys"],a:1,x:"Systems ship with trusted roots and trust certificates that chain to them, scaling trust to CAs you never directly configured."},
{q:"PKI's trust is only as strong as:",o:["The internet speed","The CAs and private-key protection — a compromised or careless CA can issue fraudulent certificates","The color of the padlock","The browser version"],a:1,x:"CA integrity and key protection at every level underpin PKI; a compromised CA undermines the whole trust chain."}]},
{t:"Certificate Authorities and Trust Chains",d:"Root, intermediate, and the chain of trust.",
obj:["Describe CA hierarchy: root and intermediate CAs","Explain the chain of trust and validation","Explain protecting the CA hierarchy"],
read:["A well-designed PKI uses a hierarchy rather than a single CA: an offline, heavily-protected root CA signs one or more intermediate CAs, which do the day-to-day certificate issuing. This two-tier (or multi-tier) structure exists for security — the root CA's private key is the ultimate trust anchor, so it is kept offline and used rarely (only to sign intermediates), while the more-exposed intermediates handle issuance. If an intermediate is compromised, it can be revoked without invalidating the root and every certificate under it; if the root were compromised, the entire PKI would collapse. Protecting the root CA (offline, in an HSM, with strict controls) is therefore paramount.",
"The chain of trust is validated at every connection: a server presents its certificate plus the intermediate(s), and the client verifies each signature up the chain to a trusted root, checking validity dates, the intended use, and — critically — that the certificate has not been revoked (module ten). A break anywhere (an expired certificate, a signature that does not validate, an untrusted root, a revoked certificate) breaks the chain and the trust. Understanding the hierarchy and validation explains common certificate errors (a missing intermediate, so the chain cannot be built; an expired certificate; an untrusted CA) and why they occur — the practical certificate troubleshooting an operator constantly does. The hierarchy also enables the internal PKI that the course's deliverable builds: an organization runs its own root and intermediate CAs to issue internal certificates."],
ex:"An organization builds a two-tier internal PKI: an offline root CA (private key in an HSM, powered on only to sign intermediates) and an online intermediate CA that issues certificates for internal services. When troubleshooting a certificate error, an operator finds the server was missing the intermediate certificate, so clients could not build the chain to the trusted root. Adding the intermediate fixed it — the chain-of-trust understanding diagnosed the common error.",
refs:[["RFC 5280 (certificate path validation)","Chain of trust validation."],["NIST SP 800-57 Part 3","Managing CA hierarchies and keys."]],
kc:[
{q:"A two-tier PKI keeps the root CA offline because:",o:["It is slow","The root is the ultimate trust anchor; keeping it offline protects it, while intermediates handle day-to-day issuance","Roots cannot sign","It saves money"],a:1,x:"The root's compromise would collapse the entire PKI, so it is kept offline and used rarely; intermediates do the exposed work."},
{q:"If an intermediate CA is compromised (vs. the root):",o:["The whole PKI collapses","It can be revoked without invalidating the root and everything under it","Nothing can be done","The root must be replaced"],a:1,x:"The hierarchy limits blast radius — a compromised intermediate is revocable, unlike a root compromise which is catastrophic."},
{q:"A 'missing intermediate certificate' error occurs because:",o:["The root is compromised","The client cannot build the chain from the server certificate up to a trusted root without the intermediate","The certificate is revoked","Encryption failed"],a:1,x:"Chain validation requires the full path; a missing intermediate breaks the chain to the trusted root — a common, fixable error."}]},
{t:"Certificate Lifecycle Management",d:"Issuing, renewing, and preventing outages.",
obj:["Describe the certificate lifecycle","Explain the risk of certificate expiration","Explain automated certificate management"],
read:["Certificates have a lifecycle — request, validation, issuance, deployment, monitoring, renewal, and eventual revocation or expiration — and managing it is a real operational discipline whose failure causes outages and security gaps. Certificates have expiration dates (increasingly short — often 90 days or a year), and an expired certificate causes an immediate, visible failure: services stop working, browsers show errors, and users are blocked. Certificate-expiration outages are common and entirely preventable, caused by nothing more than forgetting to renew — which is why certificate inventory and expiration monitoring are essential (you cannot renew a certificate you forgot you had — the inventory theme again).",
"Automation is the modern answer to lifecycle management at scale. Protocols like ACME (used by Let's Encrypt and enterprise equivalents) automate certificate issuance and renewal, eliminating the manual, error-prone process that leads to expiration outages. Automated certificate management means certificates renew themselves before expiring, are deployed consistently, and are tracked in inventory. For internal PKI, certificate-management platforms provide the same automation. The discipline is to inventory all certificates, monitor expirations, and automate renewal — turning a common outage cause into a non-event. This connects to the non-human-identity and key-management themes: certificates are credentials with lifecycles that must be managed, and unmanaged certificates are both an outage risk and a security gap (a forgotten certificate with a compromised key that is never rotated)."],
ex:"An organization suffers a major outage when a certificate on a critical service expires unnoticed — services fail and customers are blocked, all from a forgotten renewal. In response, it deploys automated certificate management (ACME-based) with full certificate inventory and expiration monitoring, so certificates now renew themselves before expiring. The common, preventable outage became a non-event — the lifecycle discipline turning a recurring failure into automation.",
refs:[["RFC 8555 (ACME)","Automated certificate issuance and renewal."],["NIST SP 800-57 Part 1","Key and certificate lifecycle management."]],
kc:[
{q:"An expired certificate causes:",o:["No effect","An immediate, visible failure — services stop working and users are blocked","Better security","Faster connections"],a:1,x:"Expiration causes outages and errors; certificate-expiration outages are common but entirely preventable with monitoring and renewal."},
{q:"The modern answer to certificate lifecycle management at scale is:",o:["Manual tracking in a spreadsheet","Automation — protocols like ACME that issue and renew certificates automatically","Ignoring expiration","Never using certificates"],a:1,x:"Automated management eliminates the manual, error-prone renewal that causes expiration outages, and keeps certificates tracked."},
{q:"You cannot renew a certificate you:",o:["Have monitored","Forgot you had — which is why certificate inventory and expiration monitoring are essential","Automated","Deployed"],a:1,x:"Unknown certificates cause surprise expiration outages; the inventory theme applies — you must know your certificates to manage them."}]},
{t:"Certificate Revocation",d:"Untrusting a certificate before it expires.",
obj:["Explain why revocation is needed","Describe CRL and OCSP mechanisms","Explain the challenges of revocation"],
read:["Sometimes a certificate must be untrusted before it expires — its private key was compromised, it was issued in error, or the identity changed. Revocation is the mechanism for this, and it is genuinely hard because clients need to know, at validation time, whether a certificate has been revoked. Two mechanisms exist: Certificate Revocation Lists (CRLs), where the CA publishes a list of revoked certificates that clients download and check, and the Online Certificate Status Protocol (OCSP), where clients query the CA in real time about a specific certificate's status. Both let a client discover that an otherwise-valid certificate should no longer be trusted.",
"Revocation has real challenges. CRLs can grow large and clients may not fetch them frequently; OCSP adds a network dependency and privacy concern (the CA learns which sites you visit) and can fail (soft-fail — if the OCSP check cannot complete, clients often proceed anyway, undermining revocation). OCSP stapling addresses some of this by having the server present a recent OCSP response, and short certificate lifetimes reduce the reliance on revocation (a certificate that expires in 90 days limits the window a compromised-but-unrevoked certificate is dangerous). The practical reality is that revocation is imperfect, which is one reason the industry has moved toward short-lived certificates — if certificates expire quickly, revocation matters less. Understanding revocation's mechanisms and limits is essential to reasoning about what happens when a certificate's key is compromised: revoke it, but also rotate to a new key, because revocation alone may not reliably reach every client."],
ex:"A server's private key is compromised. The organization revokes the certificate (via the CA, adding it to the CRL and OCSP), but knows revocation is imperfect — some clients soft-fail OCSP or have stale CRLs — so it also immediately deploys a new certificate with a fresh key pair and, given the exposure, treats the old key as fully compromised. Revocation plus key rotation and short lifetimes together manage the compromise that revocation alone might not fully contain.",
refs:[["RFC 5280 (CRL) and RFC 6960 (OCSP)","Certificate revocation mechanisms."],["CA/Browser Forum, short-lived certificates","Reducing reliance on revocation."]],
kc:[
{q:"Certificate revocation is needed when:",o:["A certificate expires normally","A certificate must be untrusted before expiration — e.g., its private key was compromised or it was issued in error","Never","Certificates are renewed"],a:1,x:"Revocation untrusts a still-valid certificate early, for key compromise, mis-issuance, or identity change."},
{q:"OCSP soft-fail undermines revocation because:",o:["It is too fast","If the status check cannot complete, clients often proceed anyway — trusting a possibly-revoked certificate","It always works","It blocks all traffic"],a:1,x:"Soft-fail means a failed OCSP check does not block the connection, so revocation may not reliably reach the client."},
{q:"Short-lived certificates reduce reliance on revocation because:",o:["They never expire","A certificate expiring in ~90 days limits the window a compromised-but-unrevoked certificate is dangerous","They cannot be revoked","Revocation is unnecessary"],a:1,x:"If certificates expire quickly, the imperfect revocation mechanisms matter less — a reason the industry moved to short lifetimes."}]},
{t:"TLS Protocol and Hardening",d:"Securing data in transit correctly.",
obj:["Explain the TLS handshake and what it establishes","Describe TLS hardening: versions, cipher suites","Explain common TLS misconfigurations"],
read:["TLS secures data in transit by combining the primitives from earlier modules: the handshake authenticates the server (via its certificate, module seven), establishes a shared symmetric key (via ephemeral key exchange, module six, for forward secrecy), and then protects the data with authenticated encryption (AEAD, module five). Understanding TLS as the assembly of these pieces demystifies it — the padlock represents a certificate-authenticated, forward-secret, authenticated-encrypted channel. TLS 1.3 (the current version) simplified and hardened the protocol, removing weak options and reducing the handshake, while TLS 1.0/1.1 and SSL are deprecated and insecure.",
"Hardening TLS means configuring it to use only strong options, because TLS supports a range of versions and cipher suites, and the weak ones are exploitable. Hardening includes: disabling deprecated protocols (SSLv3, TLS 1.0/1.1), enabling TLS 1.2 and 1.3, selecting strong cipher suites (forward-secret ECDHE, strong AEAD ciphers) and disabling weak ones (RC4, export ciphers, non-forward-secret suites), using valid certificates from trusted CAs with strong keys, and enabling features like HSTS (forcing HTTPS). Common misconfigurations — supporting deprecated protocols, weak ciphers, expired or misconfigured certificates, missing intermediates — are exactly what scanners like SSL Labs check, and they are the difference between TLS that actually protects and TLS that provides a false sense of security while remaining exploitable. The recurring theme applies: strong cryptography (TLS) is defeated by weak configuration."],
ex:"A scan of a server's TLS configuration finds it still supports TLS 1.0 and a weak non-forward-secret cipher suite — exploitable weaknesses despite also supporting TLS 1.3. Hardening it to disable the deprecated protocol and weak ciphers, keep only TLS 1.2/1.3 with forward-secret AEAD suites, and enable HSTS turns a mediocre configuration into a strong one. The certificate and encryption were fine; the weak configuration options were the vulnerability — misconfiguration defeating strong crypto.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-52 Rev.2","Guidelines for TLS implementation and configuration."],["Mozilla TLS configuration and SSL Labs","TLS hardening and testing."]],
kc:[
{q:"The TLS handshake assembles which primitives?",o:["Only symmetric encryption","Server authentication (certificate), key establishment (ephemeral exchange for PFS), and authenticated encryption","Only hashing","No cryptography"],a:1,x:"TLS combines certificate authentication, forward-secret key exchange, and AEAD — the primitives from earlier modules."},
{q:"TLS hardening includes:",o:["Supporting all protocol versions","Disabling deprecated protocols (SSLv3, TLS 1.0/1.1) and weak ciphers, keeping strong forward-secret AEAD suites","Using expired certificates","Weak keys"],a:1,x:"Hardening restricts TLS to strong versions and cipher suites, disabling the exploitable weak options."},
{q:"A server supporting TLS 1.3 but also TLS 1.0 and weak ciphers is:",o:["Fully secure","Still vulnerable — the weak options are exploitable; strong crypto is defeated by weak configuration","Best practice","Impossible"],a:1,x:"Supporting deprecated protocols and weak ciphers leaves exploitable weaknesses regardless of also supporting TLS 1.3."}]},
{t:"Key Management, HSMs, and Crypto Failures",d:"Where cryptography actually breaks.",
obj:["Explain the centrality of key management","Describe HSMs and secure key storage","Identify common cryptographic failures"],
read:["Cryptography almost never fails because the math is broken — it fails because keys are mismanaged or crypto is misapplied. Key management is the hardest and most important part of applied cryptography: keys must be generated with good randomness, stored securely (never in code or plaintext — the secrets lessons from BC-104/IC-203), distributed safely, rotated periodically, and destroyed when retired. A leaked or weak key defeats even perfect cryptography (the leaked-key breaches recur throughout this program). Hardware Security Modules (HSMs) provide the strongest key protection — tamper-resistant hardware that generates and stores keys and performs crypto operations without the keys ever leaving, used for CA roots (module eight) and high-value keys.",
"The common cryptographic failures form a catalog of misapplication: weak or broken algorithms (MD5, DES, RC4), weak keys or poor randomness, hardcoded or leaked keys, missing integrity (encryption without authentication), weak configuration (the TLS weaknesses of module twelve), improper certificate validation (accepting invalid certificates, defeating PKI), reused nonces/IVs, and rolling your own crypto instead of using vetted libraries. Each looks like it works — data is encrypted, a connection succeeds — while providing little real security. The unifying lesson of this course is that using cryptography correctly is much harder than it appears, and the failures are in the application, keys, and configuration far more than the algorithms. Using vetted implementations, managing keys rigorously (with HSMs for high-value keys), choosing current algorithms, and hardening configuration is what makes cryptography actually protect."],
ex:"An application encrypts data with strong AES but stores the encryption key hardcoded in the source code, which leaks in a repository (the IC-203 failure). The strong algorithm provided no protection because the key was exposed — cryptography failed through key mismanagement, not a broken cipher. Moving the key to an HSM or secrets manager and rotating it would have made the strong encryption actually protective. The failure, as always, was in the key management, not the math.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-57 (key management) and FIPS 140-3 (HSMs)","Key management and cryptographic module security."],["OWASP Cryptographic Failures","Common crypto misapplication in practice."]],
kc:[
{q:"Cryptography most commonly fails because:",o:["The math is broken","Keys are mismanaged or crypto is misapplied — leaked keys, broken algorithms, weak configuration","Algorithms are too strong","Encryption is too slow"],a:1,x:"The unifying lesson: failures are in application, keys, and configuration, not the algorithms — using crypto correctly is hard."},
{q:"A Hardware Security Module (HSM) provides:",o:["Faster networking","Tamper-resistant key generation and storage where keys never leave the hardware — the strongest key protection","Free certificates","Weaker security"],a:1,x:"HSMs protect high-value keys (like CA roots) in tamper-resistant hardware, performing operations without exposing the keys."},
{q:"Strong AES encryption with a hardcoded key in source code:",o:["Is secure","Provides no real protection — the leaked key defeats the strong algorithm; the failure is key management","Is best practice","Cannot be decrypted"],a:1,x:"A leaked key defeats even perfect cryptography; the failure is in key management, not the cipher — the recurring theme."}]},
{t:"Randomness and Entropy",d:"The silent foundation of all cryptography.",
obj:["Explain why randomness is critical to cryptography","Distinguish true and pseudo-random generation","Identify failures caused by weak randomness"],
read:["Cryptography depends utterly on good randomness — keys, nonces, initialization vectors, and salts must be unpredictable, because an attacker who can predict or reproduce them defeats the cryptography regardless of algorithm strength. Randomness comes from entropy (unpredictability), and cryptographic systems use cryptographically-secure pseudo-random number generators (CSPRNGs) seeded from hardware entropy sources. The distinction matters: an ordinary (non-cryptographic) random generator is predictable enough to break crypto, so cryptographic operations must use a CSPRNG, and a common vulnerability is using a weak or non-cryptographic random source for security purposes.",
"Weak randomness has caused real, severe failures. Predictable keys (from a broken random generator or insufficient entropy at startup) let attackers reproduce them; reused nonces or IVs (which must be unique) break the security of encryption modes and signature schemes catastrophically — a famous class of failures. Insufficient entropy on embedded devices or at boot has produced predictable keys across many devices. The lesson connects to the course's theme: strong algorithms with weak randomness provide no security, and the randomness failure is invisible (the system works, keys are generated) while being fatal. Using proper CSPRNGs seeded with adequate entropy, and never reusing values that must be unique, is a foundational requirement that is easy to get subtly wrong."],
ex:"A device generates its cryptographic keys at first boot before enough entropy has accumulated, producing predictable keys — and because many identical devices do the same, an attacker can reproduce the keys. The strong algorithm was defeated by weak randomness at key generation. Ensuring adequate entropy (a hardware RNG, or delaying key generation until entropy is sufficient) would have made the keys unpredictable — the invisible-but-fatal randomness failure that underlies many real crypto breaks.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-90A/B/C","Random bit generation and entropy sources."],["Real-world weak-randomness vulnerabilities (e.g., predictable keys)","How poor randomness breaks cryptography."]],
kc:[
{q:"Cryptography depends on good randomness because:",o:["Random numbers are pretty","An attacker who can predict keys, nonces, or IVs defeats the cryptography regardless of algorithm strength","Randomness speeds encryption","It is optional"],a:1,x:"Predictable or reproducible cryptographic values break the crypto; unpredictability (entropy) is foundational."},
{q:"Cryptographic operations must use:",o:["Any random function","A cryptographically-secure pseudo-random generator (CSPRNG) seeded with adequate entropy","A fixed value","No randomness"],a:1,x:"Ordinary random generators are predictable enough to break crypto; a CSPRNG with real entropy is required."},
{q:"Reusing a nonce or IV that must be unique:",o:["Improves performance","Can catastrophically break the encryption mode or signature scheme","Is required","Has no effect"],a:1,x:"Uniqueness requirements are strict; reused nonces/IVs are a famous class of catastrophic, invisible crypto failures."}]},
{t:"Cryptographic Agility and Post-Quantum",d:"Preparing for algorithms to change.",
obj:["Explain cryptographic agility and why it matters","Describe the post-quantum threat","Explain the migration challenge"],
read:["Cryptographic algorithms weaken over time — computing power grows, new attacks emerge, and standards deprecate (MD5, SHA-1, DES all followed this path). Cryptographic agility is the design property that lets a system change its algorithms without a painful rebuild: abstracting crypto behind interfaces, avoiding hardcoded algorithm choices, and maintaining an inventory of where and how cryptography is used. Systems that are not agile become stuck on deprecated, broken algorithms because changing them requires rearchitecting — the reason so many systems still use weak crypto long after it should have been retired. Agility is foresight built into design.",
"The looming driver of change is the post-quantum threat: sufficiently powerful quantum computers would break the asymmetric cryptography (RSA, ECC) that underpins PKI, TLS, and signatures, because the mathematical problems they rely on (factoring, discrete log) are efficiently solvable by quantum algorithms. NIST has standardized post-quantum cryptographic algorithms (based on different mathematical problems believed quantum-resistant), and organizations face a large, multi-year migration to them. The urgency is heightened by harvest-now-decrypt-later — adversaries recording encrypted data today to decrypt once quantum computers arrive, meaning long-term-sensitive data is already at risk. Cryptographic agility is what makes this migration feasible: systems designed to change algorithms can adopt post-quantum crypto; systems that hardcoded RSA everywhere face a painful, risky overhaul. Understanding this prepares you for the largest cryptographic transition in decades, which the advanced governance courses treat as a strategic risk."],
ex:"An organization inventories its cryptography and finds RSA hardcoded throughout its applications, with no abstraction — migrating to post-quantum algorithms would require rearchitecting dozens of systems. A second organization built crypto agility in, abstracting algorithms behind interfaces, and can adopt post-quantum crypto by updating the abstraction. When the post-quantum migration becomes urgent, agility is the difference between a manageable update and a painful, risky overhaul — foresight paying off.",
refs:[["NIST Post-Quantum Cryptography standards","The quantum-resistant algorithms and migration."],["NIST SP 1800-38 (migration to PQC)","Cryptographic agility and post-quantum transition."]],
kc:[
{q:"Cryptographic agility is the design property that:",o:["Makes crypto faster","Lets a system change its algorithms without a painful rebuild","Removes the need for keys","Uses only one algorithm forever"],a:1,x:"Agility (abstraction, no hardcoding, crypto inventory) enables changing algorithms as they weaken — foresight built into design."},
{q:"The post-quantum threat is that:",o:["Quantum computers are slow","Sufficiently powerful quantum computers would break the asymmetric crypto (RSA, ECC) underpinning PKI and TLS","Nothing changes","Symmetric crypto is broken first"],a:1,x:"Quantum algorithms efficiently solve factoring and discrete log, breaking RSA/ECC — requiring migration to post-quantum algorithms."},
{q:"'Harvest-now-decrypt-later' means adversaries:",o:["Decrypt everything instantly","Record encrypted data today to decrypt once quantum computers arrive, putting long-term-sensitive data at risk now","Cannot record traffic","Ignore encryption"],a:1,x:"Recorded encrypted data with long-term sensitivity is already at risk, adding urgency to the post-quantum migration."}]},
{t:"Cryptography Capstone",d:"Building and hardening a complete crypto deployment.",
obj:["Synthesize primitives, PKI, TLS, and key management into a deployment","Build a two-tier CA and issue/revoke certificates","Judge a crypto deployment by correct application and key management"],
read:["This capstone assembles the course into a working, hardened cryptographic deployment. Build a two-tier internal PKI (module eight): an offline root CA (key in an HSM), an online intermediate CA, and the issuance of end-entity certificates for internal services. Manage the certificate lifecycle with automation and inventory (module nine), and demonstrate revocation (module ten) by revoking a certificate and verifying clients no longer trust it. Deploy TLS on services using these certificates, hardened to strong versions and forward-secret cipher suites (module twelve), and manage all keys rigorously (module thirteen). Each piece uses the primitives (modules one through six) correctly assembled.",
"The integration demonstrates that a secure crypto deployment is the correct application of many pieces together: strong algorithms, proper key management, a sound PKI hierarchy, valid certificate lifecycle, working revocation, and hardened TLS. A weakness in any one — a broken algorithm, a leaked key, an unprotected root CA, an expired certificate, a weak TLS configuration — undermines the whole, because cryptography's strength is limited by its weakest link. The capstone requires all the pieces working together, which is why applied cryptography is genuinely hard and so often gets subtly wrong in ways that look fine.",
"The graded deliverable — building a two-tier internal certificate authority hierarchy and issuing and revoking test certificates — exercises exactly this PKI competency at the heart of the course. Its quality is judged by whether the PKI is correctly structured (protected offline root, proper chain), the certificates are properly issued and validated, and revocation actually works. An organization that manages cryptography correctly — right algorithms, rigorous keys, sound PKI, hardened TLS — gets the real protection cryptography promises; one that applies it carelessly gets a false sense of security while remaining exploitable, the outcome this entire course exists to prevent.",],
ex:"A team builds the capstone deployment: an offline root CA in an HSM, an online intermediate issuing service certificates, automated lifecycle management, working revocation (verified by revoking a test certificate and confirming clients reject it), and hardened TLS on the services. A crypto assessment finds correct algorithms, protected keys, a sound chain, and strong configuration throughout. The integrated deployment provides real protection because every piece was applied correctly — the standard that separates protective cryptography from a false sense of security.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-57 and SP 800-52 Rev.2","Key management and TLS in a complete deployment."],["Cryptography Engineering","Applying cryptography correctly end to end."]],
kc:[
{q:"A secure cryptographic deployment requires:",o:["Just a strong algorithm","The correct application of many pieces — strong algorithms, key management, sound PKI, valid lifecycle, working revocation, hardened TLS","Only a certificate","Only encryption"],a:1,x:"Cryptography's strength is limited by its weakest link; a secure deployment integrates all the pieces correctly."},
{q:"The capstone PKI deployment is judged by:",o:["The number of certificates","Correct structure (protected offline root, proper chain), proper issuance/validation, and working revocation","Its speed","The CA vendor"],a:1,x:"A sound PKI has a protected root, valid chains, proper issuance, and functioning revocation — the marks of correct application."},
{q:"An organization that applies cryptography carelessly gets:",o:["Real protection","A false sense of security while remaining exploitable — the outcome this course exists to prevent","Better performance","Nothing at all"],a:1,x:"Careless crypto looks like it works (data encrypted, connections succeed) while providing little real security — the core warning."}]}
],
cap:[
{q:"SCENARIO: Data is encrypted in transit, but an attacker who cannot read it still alters it harmfully. The missing property was:",o:["A stronger key","Integrity — encryption alone does not detect alteration; authenticated encryption (AEAD/MAC) was needed","A longer certificate","More encryption"],a:1,x:"Encryption provides confidentiality but not integrity; authenticated encryption is required to get both."},
{q:"SCENARIO: An encrypted image still visibly shows the original's outline. The cause is:",o:["A weak algorithm","ECB mode — identical blocks produce identical ciphertext, leaking patterns; a secure mode like GCM is needed","No key","A broken certificate"],a:1,x:"The mode, not the algorithm, leaked structure; ECB is insecure and secure modes like GCM avoid the pattern leakage."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A system verifies software integrity with MD5, and an attacker crafts a malicious file with a matching MD5. The lesson is:",o:["Hashing is useless","Using a broken hash (MD5) provides no real security — collision resistance is defeated; use SHA-256","MD5 is fine","Integrity is impossible"],a:1,x:"Broken algorithms silently undermine security; MD5's broken collision resistance defeats the integrity check."},
{q:"SCENARIO: An attacker records encrypted traffic, later steals the server's private key, but cannot decrypt the past traffic. This is because the server used:",o:["A weak cipher","Ephemeral key exchange providing perfect forward secrecy","Static keys","No encryption"],a:1,x:"PFS from ephemeral key exchange means a future key compromise cannot retroactively decrypt recorded past sessions."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A certificate error shows clients cannot build the chain to a trusted root. The likely cause is:",o:["The root is compromised","A missing intermediate certificate — the chain cannot reach the trusted root without it","Weak encryption","OCSP soft-fail"],a:1,x:"Chain validation requires the full path; a missing intermediate is a common, fixable cause of certificate errors."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A major outage occurs because a certificate on a critical service expired unnoticed. The prevention is:",o:["Longer certificates only","Certificate inventory, expiration monitoring, and automated renewal (ACME)","Ignoring certificates","Manual tracking"],a:1,x:"Expiration outages are preventable with inventory, monitoring, and automated renewal — you cannot renew what you forgot you had."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A server's private key is compromised. Revocation alone may not fully protect because:",o:["Revocation always works","Revocation is imperfect (OCSP soft-fail, stale CRLs), so the key must also be rotated and treated as fully compromised","Keys cannot be revoked","Nothing can be done"],a:1,x:"Because revocation may not reach every client reliably, key rotation and short lifetimes are needed alongside revocation."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A TLS scan shows a server supporting TLS 1.3 but also TLS 1.0 and a weak cipher. This means the server is:",o:["Fully secure","Still vulnerable — the weak options are exploitable; strong crypto is defeated by weak configuration","Impossible to attack","Best-configured"],a:1,x:"Supporting deprecated protocols and weak ciphers leaves exploitable weaknesses; hardening must disable them."},
{q:"SCENARIO: An app uses strong AES but hardcodes the key in source code, which leaks. The failure was:",o:["A weak algorithm","Key management — a leaked key defeats even perfect cryptography","Too much encryption","A bad certificate"],a:1,x:"Cryptography fails through key mismanagement, not the math; the exposed key defeats the strong cipher."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A crypto deployment is judged secure. The correct standard is:",o:["It uses AES","Every piece applied correctly — strong algorithms, rigorous key management, sound PKI, working revocation, hardened TLS","It has a certificate","It is fast"],a:1,x:"Cryptography's strength is its weakest link; a secure deployment integrates all pieces correctly, not just one strong element."}
]};
